3. Smallest-area triangle, empty wedges, and other applications. 
Let S = {Pl ..... p,} be a set of n points in the plane. We will apply 
Theorem 1 to the problem of determining which of the (] ) triangles with vertices 
in S has the smallest a"rea. The difficulty of this problem resides essentially in 
the absence of locality. Indeed, the vertices of the smallest triangle may be 
arbitrarily distant from one another, if they happen to be collinear (this illustrates 
the fact that solving this problem will allow us to determine whether three points 
in S are collinear). Using duality, we are able to strenghten the time complexity 
of the best algorithm known for this problem, i.e., the O(n 2 logn) time and O(n) 
space algorithm of [8]. 
